-- title:
--   Failure bound for one adaptively selected integer draw
-- statement:
--   Fix a finite Boolean base prefix $h$, a horizon $t\in\mathbb N$, and a trial cap $T\in\mathbb N$. An admissible draw history consists of a finite prefix-free family $H$ of relative stopping prefixes of length at most $t$, and a positive denominator $v(p)$ determined by each $p\in H$. Assume
--
--   $$T\bigl(\operatorname{log}_2(v(p)-1)+1\bigr)\le t-|p|\qquad(p\in H).$$
--
--   Let $A$ be the set of length-$t$ strings $b$ for which some $p\in H$ prefixes $b$ and the bounded draw with denominator $v(p)$ and cap $T$ fails at initial cursor $|h|+|p|$ on the zero-extended tape $h\mathbin{+\!+}b$. Then
--
--   $$\#A\,2^{-t}\le2^{-T}.$$
--
--   One adaptive draw therefore retains the fixed-denominator bound when its denominator is determined before its fresh trial bits and all trials are covered. The formal mass lies in the extended nonnegative reals.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/meelgroup/counting-matroid/blob/dd16a4628fdb095c58fe0f3dfd8e353c6e97443c/CountingMatroid/Analysis/BoundedUniformAbortMass.lean#L313-L357

